nodecode-trove is a rewrite of misterdas's misterdas/hermes-trove:

  https://github.com/misterdas/hermes-trove
  commit 69c673eeb2fcbaf12e9b88451ad1f627a88bc930
  MIT, Copyright (c) 2026 Stephen Schoettler (LICENSE)

Rewritten: the original's model-facing reads, as six tools over a log this
image already keeps. trove_grep and semantic trove_recall became
trove_search (one query, every session at once, newest first, each row
cited by [session seq]); trove_recent and trove_load_session became
trove_read (one session in order, paged by before=); trove_expand became
trove_entry (one seq, body whole); trove_describe became trove_sessions;
trove_compile_evidence and trove_evidence_pack became trove_evidence (the
matched rows as one digest, duplicates dropped, cut to a character budget);
trove_status and trove_doctor became trove_status; and the original's
slash command became /trove with a panel.

Left out, and why: the original's own store. It keeps every message in a
SQLite DAG of its own, with FTS, summaries, embeddings and a compaction
policy. Nodecode's durable event log already holds the same facts and its
eviction only moves a visibility floor, so a second store would duplicate
what the read is for; the cell reads the log through Nodecode's own recall
seams and keeps nothing. With that store went semantic recall (query
embeddings, vector search, reranking, and the optional call to an embedding
provider), the summary and rollup DAG, assertion extraction and the AMR
evidence compiler, the externalized sidecar payloads, presets and the
model-routing table, session patterns and time-range filters, and the
doctor's schema-integrity checks - a JSON-shaped index and a SQLite schema
are not things this read has. What survives is the part a user notices: a
message is never lost, a row is exact, and the seq it cites still opens the
body later.
